# Cursor IDE Rules - Spring Boot Billing Consumer Service

## Code Style and Standards

### Java Code Rules
- Use Java 21 features
- Follow Spring Boot 3.x best practices
- Use Lombok annotations (@RequiredArgsConstructor, @Getter, @Setter, @Builder)
- Use SLF4J for logging (never System.out.println or printStackTrace)
- All service methods that modify data must have @Transactional
- All controller request parameters must have @Valid annotation
- Use Optional for nullable return types

### Security Rules (CRITICAL)
- NEVER hardcode secrets, passwords, API keys, or tokens
- Always use @Value("${ENV_VAR}") or environment variables
- Sensitive fields (password, ssn, medical data) must use @Convert with encryption
- All SQL queries must use JPA/Hibernate - no string concatenation
- All external inputs must be validated with @Valid, @NotNull, @NotBlank

### Exception Handling
- Never use empty catch blocks
- Never catch generic Exception unless in GlobalExceptionHandler
- Always log exceptions with log.error("message", exception)
- Use try-with-resources for all closeable resources

### Architecture Rules
- Controllers only handle HTTP concerns - delegate to Services
- Services contain business logic - use @Transactional
- Repositories only for data access
- Never expose Entity classes in API responses - always use DTOs
- All API endpoints must be versioned: /api/v1/...

### Naming Conventions
- Classes: PascalCase (BillingConsumerService)
- Methods: camelCase (createConsumer)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (MAX_RETRY_COUNT)
- Packages: lowercase (com.summithc.billing)
- Test methods: methodName_scenario_expectedResult

### Code Quality
- Maximum method length: 50 lines
- Methods should do one thing only
- Avoid deeply nested code (max 3 levels)
- Use meaningful variable names (no single letters except loop counters)
- Add null checks or use Optional to prevent NullPointerException

### Configuration
- All environment-specific values must use ${ENV_VAR:default}
- Never commit secrets in application.yml or properties files
- Use datasource-secrets.yml (gitignored) for local database credentials

### Testing
- Unit tests must not connect to real databases (use H2 or mocks)
- Test coverage minimum 80% for Service layer
- Use @DataJpaTest for repository tests
- Use @MockBean for service tests

### Documentation
- Public API endpoints must have clear Javadoc
- Complex business logic must have explanatory comments
- README must be updated when adding new features

### Git Commit Messages
- Follow conventional commits: type(scope): description
- Valid types: feat, fix, docs, refactor, test, chore, style, perf, ci, build
- Examples: "feat(billing): add consumer creation API"

### Dependencies
- Never use SNAPSHOT versions in pom.xml
- Keep dependencies updated to avoid security vulnerabilities
- Only add necessary dependencies - avoid bloat

## When Writing Code

When I ask you to write or modify code:
1. Check these rules first
2. Apply security rules strictly
3. Use proper error handling
4. Write clean, maintainable code
5. Add appropriate logging
6. Follow the architecture patterns established in this project

